From 7c8caba91161fbbc2a0518b85d9eb1074b3cef21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 17:02:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give incognito its extension background pages back

A split-mode extension allowed in incognito never ran there. uBlock Origin
declares "incognito": "split", the Allow in Incognito toggle in
chrome://extensions set the preference correctly, and nothing filtered: a
page that blocks two requests in a normal tab blocked none in an incognito
one.

Nothing was refusing the work. IncognitoProcessManager::CreateBackgroundHost
already agrees to build a host for a split-mode extension that is enabled in
incognito, and it was simply never called, because the only route to it is
MaybeCreateStartupBackgroundHosts and nobody asked the incognito manager to
run it.

The ProcessManager constructor listens for the extension system becoming
ready, but only when the context is on the record. Off the record it stays
silent, and the comment explains why: on desktop the embedder does the
asking. ChromeProcessManagerDelegate::OnBrowserCreated calls
MaybeCreateStartupBackgroundHosts as soon as an incognito window appears,
and OnProfileAdded covers the deferred case.

Android has no such embedder. chrome_process_manager_delegate.cc is not
compiled there and GetProcessManagerDelegate returns nullptr, so the call
never came from anywhere and the incognito manager waited for a window that
this platform does not have.

So the incognito manager now listens too, but only where there is no
delegate to do it instead. A platform that has one behaves exactly as
before. This does not hand spanning extensions a second background page:
the incognito manager overrides CreateBackgroundHost and builds one only
for split-mode extensions that the user has allowed in incognito.

Tested on a Pixel 3 with a page that blocks two requests. Allowed in
incognito it now blocks both there as well as in a normal tab; turned off,
incognito blocks none while normal browsing still blocks both; turned back
on, incognito blocks both again.
---
 extensions/browser/process_manager.cc | 21 ++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/extensions/browser/process_manager.cc b/extensions/browser/process_manager.cc
index d097a45600c..1bccd8e56ef 100644
--- a/extensions/browser/process_manager.cc
+++ b/extensions/browser/process_manager.cc
@@ -210,9 +210,24 @@ ProcessManager::ProcessManager(BrowserContext* context,
       last_background_close_sequence_id_(0) {
   extension_registry_->AddObserver(this);
 
-  // Only the original profile needs to listen for ready to create background
-  // pages for all spanning extensions.
-  if (!context->IsOffTheRecord()) {
+  // The original profile listens for ready so that spanning extensions get
+  // their background pages.
+  //
+  // An incognito profile normally does not, because on desktop the embedder
+  // tells it when to start: ChromeProcessManagerDelegate::OnBrowserCreated
+  // calls MaybeCreateStartupBackgroundHosts once an incognito window exists.
+  // Android has no such delegate at all, GetProcessManagerDelegate returns
+  // nullptr there, so nothing ever made that call and a split-mode extension
+  // allowed in incognito never got a background page: the preference was on,
+  // IncognitoProcessManager::CreateBackgroundHost would have agreed, and it
+  // was simply never reached. Listening here too covers the embedder that has
+  // nobody to ask. Where a delegate does exist the behaviour is unchanged.
+  //
+  // This does not give spanning extensions a second background page. The
+  // incognito manager overrides CreateBackgroundHost and creates one only for
+  // split-mode extensions that are enabled in incognito.
+  if (!context->IsOffTheRecord() ||
+      !ExtensionsBrowserClient::Get()->GetProcessManagerDelegate()) {
     ExtensionSystem::Get(context)->ready().Post(
         FROM_HERE,
         base::BindOnce(&ProcessManager::MaybeCreateStartupBackgroundHosts,
